  "findings": "Comparing 10 replicates at baseline concurrency=2 (below the 5-connection capacity) against 10 replicates at elevated concurrency=30 (6x capacity): both p50 and p99 shifted (Cliff's Delta -1.00 and -1.00, both large), but the p99-minus-p50 spread also grew (median difference 3.13ms, 95% CI [1.76,4.65]) -- the tail didn't just move with the rest of the distribution, it stretched further from the median than at baseline, the signature of queueing-induced variance rather than a uniform shift. Decomposing the elevated p99 (137.36ms) against the baseline p99 (22.77ms) as an estimate of the fixed service+overhead component: 83% of the elevated p99 is attributable to waiting time, not service time, which did not change (Origin's configured delay was identical, 20ms, in both conditions) -- this decomposition is only trustworthy because the service component was independently known and held constant by construction, not inferred from the latency data itself."
